1 REM ============================================================
2 REM  dialect_sbasic.bpp
3 REM  SUPER BASIC (Tymshare SDS-940, 1968) Dialect Test
4 REM ============================================================
5 REM
6 REM  SUPER BASIC was developed by Mark Alexander and Dan Streng
7 REM  at Tymshare, Inc. for the Scientific Data Systems (SDS) 940
8 REM  mainframe computer, circa 1968.
9 REM
10 REM  Tymshare was a pioneering time-sharing company that allowed
11 REM  multiple users to access a central mainframe via teletype
12 REM  terminals connected over telephone lines.
13 REM
14 REM  SUPER BASIC was one of the most advanced BASIC dialects
15 REM  of its era, adding:
16 REM    - Complex number arithmetic
17 REM    - Double precision via # suffix
18 REM    - Extended math (ASIN, ACOS, SINH, COSH, TANH, etc.)
19 REM    - Comparison functions (COMP, PDIF)
20 REM    - PI constant
21 REM    - JOSS-style postfix modifiers
22 REM    - FOR...BY...TO ordering (step before limit)
23 REM    - Enhanced matrix operations
24 REM
25 REM  Dialect code: "SBAS"
26 REM
100 REM === Dialect Selection ===
101 REM Use DIALECT "SBAS" to enter SUPER BASIC mode.
102 REM The prompt changes to "READY" and the max line
103 REM number extends to 99999.
110 DIALECT "SBAS"
120 TEST "Dialect SBAS active"
130 ASSERT 1=1
140 ENDTEST
200 REM === Extended Math Functions ===
201 REM SUPER BASIC introduced inverse trig functions
202 REM that were not available in microcomputer BASICs.
210 TEST "ASIN/ACOS inverse trig"
220 ASSERT ABS(ASIN(0)) < 0.0001
230 ASSERT ABS(ACOS(1)) < 0.0001
240 ASSERT ABS(ASIN(1) - ACOS(0)) < 0.0001
250 ENDTEST
300 REM === Hyperbolic Functions ===
301 REM SINH, COSH, TANH are the hyperbolic counterparts
302 REM of SIN, COS, TAN. They appear in engineering,
303 REM physics, and the shape of hanging cables (catenary).
310 TEST "SINH/COSH/TANH hyperbolics"
320 ASSERT ABS(SINH(0)) < 0.0001
330 ASSERT ABS(COSH(0) - 1) < 0.0001
340 ASSERT ABS(TANH(0)) < 0.0001
350 REM Identity: cosh^2 - sinh^2 = 1
360 ASSERT ABS(COSH(1)^2 - SINH(1)^2 - 1) < 0.0001
370 ENDTEST
400 REM === Logarithms ===
401 REM LOG10 = common log (base 10)
402 REM LOG2  = binary log (base 2)
403 REM The standard LOG function returns natural log (base e).
410 TEST "LOG10 and LOG2"
420 ASSERT ABS(LOG10(100) - 2) < 0.0001
430 ASSERT ABS(LOG2(8) - 3) < 0.0001
440 ENDTEST
500 REM === COMP and PDIF ===
501 REM COMP(a,b) returns -1, 0, or +1 (three-way compare).
502 REM PDIF(a,b) returns MAX(a-b, 0) (positive difference).
503 REM These are common in numerical algorithms.
510 TEST "COMP and PDIF"
520 ASSERT COMP(3, 5) = -1
530 ASSERT COMP(5, 5) = 0
540 ASSERT COMP(7, 3) = 1
550 ASSERT PDIF(10, 3) = 7
560 ASSERT PDIF(3, 10) = 0
570 ENDTEST
600 REM === PI Constant ===
601 REM PI returns 3.14159265358979...
602 REM In SUPER BASIC, PI was a zero-argument function.
610 TEST "PI constant"
620 ASSERT ABS(PI - 3.14159) < 0.001
630 REM Verify identity: sin(PI/2) = 1
640 ASSERT ABS(SIN(PI / 2) - 1) < 0.0001
650 ENDTEST
700 REM === Complex Numbers ===
701 REM SUPER BASIC natively supported complex arithmetic.
702 REM In BASIC++, complex numbers use COMPLEX(real, imag)
703 REM and are extracted with REAL() and IMAG().
710 TEST "Complex numbers (SBASIC)"
720 LET Z = COMPLEX(3, 4)
730 ASSERT REAL(Z) = 3
740 ASSERT IMAG(Z) = 4
750 ENDTEST
800 REM === MIN/MAX (originated in SUPER BASIC) ===
801 REM MIN and MAX were standard SUPER BASIC functions.
802 REM They accept 2+ arguments and return the smallest
803 REM or largest value.
810 TEST "MIN/MAX (SBASIC origin)"
820 ASSERT MIN(5, 3) = 3
830 ASSERT MAX(5, 3) = 5
840 ASSERT MIN(1, 2, 3, 4, 5) = 1
850 ASSERT MAX(1, 2, 3, 4, 5) = 5
860 ENDTEST
900 REM === Combined Usage ===
901 REM These functions combine naturally in expressions,
902 REM just as they did on the Tymshare mainframe.
910 TEST "Combined expressions"
920 REM Convert 45 degrees to radians using PI
930 LET RAD45 = PI / 4
940 ASSERT ABS(SIN(RAD45) - 0.7071) < 0.001
950 REM Verify ASIN(SIN(x)) = x
960 ASSERT ABS(ASIN(SIN(RAD45)) - RAD45) < 0.0001
970 ENDTEST
1000 REM === UNLESS (JOSS-Style Negated IF) ===
1001 REM SUPER BASIC borrowed UNLESS from JOSS (1963).
1002 REM UNLESS is the negated form of IF:
1003 REM   UNLESS condition THEN statement
1004 REM executes when the condition is FALSE.
1005 REM
1006 REM This is equivalent to: IF NOT condition THEN statement
1010 TEST "UNLESS statement"
1020 LET R = 0
1030 UNLESS 1=2 THEN LET R = 1
1040 ASSERT R = 1
1050 REM UNLESS with true condition should NOT execute
1060 LET R = 0
1070 UNLESS 1=1 THEN LET R = 99
1080 ASSERT R = 0
1090 ENDTEST
1100 REM === FOR...BY (Alternative to STEP) ===
1101 REM In SUPER BASIC, BY was the keyword for step size.
1102 REM   FOR I = 1 TO 10 BY 2
1103 REM is equivalent to:
1104 REM   FOR I = 1 TO 10 STEP 2
1110 TEST "FOR...BY keyword"
1120 LET S = 0
1130 FOR I = 1 TO 5 BY 2
1140 LET S = S + I
1150 NEXT I
1160 REM 1 + 3 + 5 = 9
1170 ASSERT S = 9
1180 ENDTEST
9999 END
